acceptance_criteria:
First slice = gate-proxy core + the /api/-/gate hypermedia door for the EMAIL tokens only (email.read / email.send / email.move), with the Gate MCP client session and the tool→capability map. A conformance test proving:
  • the affordance document shows ONLY what the presented grant admits (a grant with email.read sees the read links; a grant without email.send does NOT see the send form);
  • an ungranted invoke is refused (403/404) and never reaches Gate;
  • a granted read returns Gate's payload, and a granted send forwards and returns Gate's payload;
  • no rows are written — nothing is mirrored or stored.
Once proven, the other rigs (telegram, messages, ynab, amazon) are just additional rows in the tool→capability map — no new code shape.

description:
waymark-iqa.4 landed the tickler kind and its feed population; what it did NOT land is anything that CREATES a marker for the 25 dropped tasks the epic is about. A tickler is born at its own create door — a person setting something aside, or an agent at the MCP door sweeping the pile, which is the dispatch-probe pattern spec-feed already blesses for the compiler.

That is an honest v1 (the same posture .6's compiler takes) but it means the epic's opening number — 25 dropped of 113 — is not yet a someday/maybe list until somebody sweeps it once.

Three reasons a birth-time sweep had nowhere to live in .4, all worth re-reading before picking a shape:
- the feed is a GET, and a read that minted rows would be a read with a side effect;
- mirror :on-gone takes a {:set …} map, not a hook, so a task dropping cannot birth a marker;
- the :decision sugar refuses an :on-create beside it (one home per hook), and composing the two would mint a wrapper fn, which resource.clj records as 'a hash that moves for nothing'.
